        "brent": 97.23,
        "brentDeltaPct": -2.39,
        "bullets": [
            "Unverified reports claim the Strait of Hormuz is closed, a development not independently confirmed by US or CENTCOM sources.",
            "PortWatch recorded 15 transits on 2026-07-19, against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day, as tanker crossings fall to their lowest level in over two months per gCaptain.",
            "Brent stood at $97.23, down 2.39% over 24 hours as of 14:52 UTC, while port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports in the most recent 24 hours reached 455 vessels."
        ],
        "date": "2026-07-24",
        "generatedAt": "2026-07-24T14:52:24.535Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz is at the center of an acute operational crisis, with unverified single-source reporting claiming a formal closure; US and CENTCOM sources have not confirmed that claim, and it must be treated as unconfirmed. Physical throughput data independently supports the severity of the disruption: PortWatch's most recently published day, 2026-07-19, recorded 15 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day, and gCaptain reports tanker crossings fell to just one vessel on Thursday, the lowest reading since May. Port arrivals across Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides totaled 455 in the most recent 24 hours, a separate methodology not comparable to the PortWatch transit count; separately, our AIS tracking showed 379 vessels anchored or stopped as of 14:52 UTC. Brent slid to $97.23, off 2.39% over 24 hours as of 14:52 UTC, with price pressure reflecting competing forces: physical supply disruption pulling prices up against demand-destruction fears pulling them down. The Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor. The Escalation Forecast composite reads high and is rising, driven primarily by Polymarket escalation contract pricing. Those two composites diverge in direction, with the state measure flat while the forward-looking forecast climbs. A US ceasefire proposal transmitted through the Iraqi prime minister was rejected by Tehran, per International Business Times, and Kpler's Matt Smith told CNBC the strait is not expected to reopen until next year. CENTCOM struck Iranian military sites amid the tensions, per Crypto Briefing, an unverified single-source report.",
